"ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-4160-8894"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Division of Pulmonary Sciences and Critical Care Medicine, University of Colorado-AMC, Aurora, CO, 80045, USA."}, {"affiliation": "Department of Cell and Developmental Biology, University of Colorado-AMC, Aurora, CO, 80045, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Seibold MA", "lastName": "Seibold", "firstName": "Max A", "initials": "MA", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8685-4263"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Center for Genes, Environment, and Health, National Jewish Health, Denver, CO, 80206, USA. CONTACT_EMAIL."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-6944-6252"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-8774-7454"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-9598-7298"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-1935-4672"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-4160-8894"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-6748-2329"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-8685-4263"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0003-2240-8632"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data", "related_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "11", "journalIssueId": 2949307, "dateOfPublication": "2020 May", "monthOfPublication": 5, "yearOfPublication": 2020, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Nat Commun", "title": "Nature communications", "essn": "2041-1723", "issn": "2041-1723", "isoabbreviation": "Nat Commun", "nlmid": "101528555"}, "printPublicationDate": "2020-05-01"}, "pubYear": "2020", "pageInfo": "2485", "abstractText": "Cigarette smoke first interacts with the lung through the cellularly diverse airway epithelium and goes on to drive development of most chronic lung diseases. Here, through single cell RNA-sequencing analysis of the tracheal epithelium from smokers and non-smokers, we generate a comprehensive atlas of epithelial cell types and states, connect these into lineages, and define cell-specific responses to smoking. Our analysis infers multi-state lineages that develop into surface mucus secretory and ciliated cells and then contrasts these to the unique specification of submucosal gland (SMG) cells. Accompanying knockout studies reveal that tuft-like cells are the likely progenitor of both pulmonary neuroendocrine cells and CFTR-rich ionocytes. Our smoking analysis finds that all cell types, including protected stem and SMG populations, are affected by smoking through both pan-epithelial smoking response networks and hundreds of cell-specific response genes, redefining the penetrance and cellular specificity of smoking effects on the human airway epithelium.", "affiliation": "Center for Genes, Environment, and Health, National Jewish Health, Denver, CO, 80206, USA.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Cystic Fibrosis Foundation", "grantId": "BRATCH16I0", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "HL107202", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Jewish Health Regenerative Medicine and Genome Editing Program", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "P01 HL132821", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "R01 HL128439", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "HL132821", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "U.S. Department of Health &amp; Human Services | National Institutes of Health", "grantId": "MD010443", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NHLBI NIH HHS", "acronym": "HL", "grantId": "P01 HL107202", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIMHD NIH HHS", "acronym": "MD", "grantId": "R01 MD010443", "orderIn": 0